<Layout title="About the Festival | Breck Sky Fest" description="Breckenridge Sky Festival is a Summit County dark-sky celebration organized by AstroTours.org, with grant support from the Town of Breckenridge. November 2026.">
  <PageIntro eyebrow="Meet Breck Sky Fest" title="Breck looks up." description="A Summit County dark-sky celebration centered in Breckenridge, bringing together astronomy, hands-on science, and local creativity in November 2026." accent="orange" icon="planet"/>
  <section class="section paper-section"><div class="wrap feature-split"><div><span class="eyebrow">The first edition</span><h2>A festival<br/>built on curiosity.</h2><p class="large">Breck Sky Fest is being developed by AstroTours.org with grant support from the Town of Breckenridge. The first festival is in November 2026, with room for schools, artists, businesses, and community groups across Summit County to take part.</p><p>The core program in development includes school visits, free community stargazing, public solar astronomy, and the ticketed High Country Stargaze. The first round of invitations has gone out to education, arts, film, hospitality, outdoor-access, and dark-sky contacts. Additional hosts and activities will be announced as participation is agreed.</p><p>Exact dates, venues, and individual events are still being arranged. Final details will be posted here in October, with unconfirmed program elements clearly marked until then.</p><a class="text-link" href="/program/">See what’s taking shape <Icon name="arrow" size={18}/></a></div><figure class="editorial-photo"><img src="/images/friends-telescope.webp" alt="A group smiling beside an AstroTours telescope at an evening program" width="1280" height="960" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Good company at a previous AstroTours program.</figcaption></figure></div></section>
  <section class="section dark-section"><div class="wrap intro-split"><div><span class="eyebrow">Why the night sky?</span><h2>There’s a lot<br/>worth looking up for.</h2></div><div><p class="large">You don’t need a science background to be curious about a planet, a distant star, or the light that reaches us from another galaxy.</p><p>The program connects telescope viewing with hands-on science, creative workshops, and opportunities to learn about the night sky.</p><p>Dark skies are part of the festival’s focus, too. We want guests to experience the night and learn how thoughtful lighting can help protect our view of it.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="section paper-section"><div class="wrap feature-split"><figure class="editorial-photo"><img src="/images/evening-telescope.webp" alt="A large telescope ready for an AstroTours evening session beside a warmly lit building" width="1280" height="960" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>A previous AstroTours setup. Festival venues are TBA.</figcaption></figure><div><span class="eyebrow">The people behind the telescopes</span><h2>Organized by<br/>AstroTours.org.</h2><p>Breck Sky Fest is led by astronomer Luke Huxley and AstroTours.org, which brings guided telescope experiences to communities, schools, visitors, and private groups in Colorado.</p><p>AstroTours is developing the core astronomy sessions. Other organizations can lead their own activities, contribute a station, host an event, or support the practical costs.</p><div class="actions"><a href="https://www.astrotours.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="button button-dark">Meet AstroTours.org <Icon name="diagonal" size={18}/></a><a href="/get-involved/" class="text-link">Get involved <Icon name="arrow" size={18}/></a></div></div></div></section>
  <Updates/>
</Layout>
